<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Castelvecchio (Verona)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Castelvecchio Bridge, Ponte Scaligero</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Castelvecchio is a fortress in Verona built from 1354 by Cangrande II della Scala, together with the fortified Ponte Scaligero across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adige">Adige</a>. Later a barracks, it was restored in the 20th century and converted by the architect Carlo Scarpa into the Castelvecchio Museum. The bridge, blown up by retreating German troops in 1945, was rebuilt from its original materials.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/castelvecchio-verona.jpg" alt="Castelvecchio (Verona)" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arne Müseler · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italy_-_Verona_-_Ponte_Scaligero.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Castelvecchio, the Old Castle, is the most important military building of the Scaliger dynasty that ruled Verona in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. It is a square, compact compound of red brick with very little decoration, one of the prominent examples of Gothic architecture of its age, with M-shaped merlons along the walls of the castle and its bridge. It has seven towers and a raised keep, the maschio, with four main buildings inside, and it is ringed by a ditch, now dry, once filled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adige">Adige</a>. It stands on the probable site of a Roman fortress outside the Roman city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Cangrande II della Scala had the castle and its bridge built as a deterrent to powerful neighbours such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the Gonzaga. Construction ran from 1354 to 1376, though Cangrande died in 1359. The fortified bridge was meant to let the lords escape northwards to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> in case of rebellion, since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scaliger">Scaligeri</a> were allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and when the family lost Verona its surviving members left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and founded a German branch.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Under Venetian rule the bridge was further fortified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>. French troops damaged the castle in 1796–7 in reprisal for the Pasque Veronesi, a violent revolt of the city's people, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> stayed there on his visits to Verona. The Austrians turned it into barracks. It was restored in 1923 and again in 1963–1965, and the bridge, destroyed by the retreating German army in 1945, was rebuilt in 1949. The castle now houses the Castelvecchio Museum and the local officers' club.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Castelvecchio (Verona)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The family's Gothic tombs, the Arche Scaligere, and the <b>Castelvecchio</b> bridge survive.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-verona">Lordship of Verona</a></li></ul></section>
